From 23f282a034a8211b57fcae505482ef05db05f39e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: i <v@g>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 18:48:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] assurance.watch: draft the Article 14 intake, and decide
 nothing
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The watch finds a stop-use advisory against a still machine at 09:00. Then
nothing happened. Somebody read the output, opened a spreadsheet, and started
working out which Member States the affected serial numbers went to — field 5
of the ENISA form, and the reason a 24-hour deadline is hard rather than short.

This closes the gap and stops exactly where honesty requires it.

It assembles the intake from evidence that already exists: the signal, the
affected product versions, and the Member States built from serial numbers in
the fleet. It records when the signal was received, because the interval
between receipt and awareness is the number a market surveillance authority asks
about first and cannot be reconstructed afterwards.

It establishes no awareness and chooses no track. Art. 14(2)(a) runs twenty-four
hours from awareness, not from receipt, and a tool that quietly equated the two
would either start a manufacturer's clock early — inventing a filing obligation
— or start it late, which is worse. C(2026) 5252 Annex §213 defines awareness as
a reasonable degree of certainty after an initial assessment and §214 requires
that assessment to be prompt; the word "prompt" is doing the work, and only a
person can be prompt. srp_prefill still omits notification_type and
awareness_datetime.

So what it reports instead is the signal nobody assessed. If a previous run
recorded receipt and no awareness record has appeared since, every later run
says so and says how long it has been, with the provision that makes it matter.
Nobody is told they are late — the window runs from awareness and awareness has
not been established, so it is deliberately not called a breach. That is the
alarm worth paying for: not "you have a deadline", which everybody knows, but
"this one has been sitting for thirty-nine hours and nobody has made the
determination".

Receipt is carried forward between runs rather than recomputed. Resetting it
every run would turn the one number an authority asks about first into a number
that is always small. And the prompt stops the moment a person records
awareness: from there the register holds the clock, and a second system nagging
about a decision already taken is how people learn to close the tab.

The duty is declared, never inferred. Only a manufacturer placing the product on
the Union market carries it; an integrator installing somebody else's cells may
or may not be one, and that is a legal question about a role. While article_14
is absent from the configuration nothing is drafted at all, which is the safe
direction to be wrong in. Declaring it without naming who receives signals is
refused, because an unattributed receipt cannot evidence the interval.

The CLI prints confirmed and unconfirmed separately and always. "0 machines" on
its own reads as "you are fine"; in the worked case it means the supplier
published no artefact hashes, so nothing is confirmed and field 5 cannot be
completed — the opposite of fine. Same argument SUPPLIER.md makes to suppliers,
arriving from the other end.

Tests: 15 new, 734 total, all passing. ruff clean, mypy clean on the new module.
The load-bearing ones assert that no duty declared drafts nothing, that the
prefill never contains awareness_datetime or notification_type, that a
label-only match confirms nothing and says so, that thirty-nine hours unassessed
escalates while three hours does not, and that recording real awareness in the
register stops the prompt.

